# Drive a system that `detc` converges, from somewhere else.
#
# Every method is a subcommand of `detc`, run on the machine that answers, and
# every reply carries one line of what that subcommand prints.  The methods
# marked below as streaming answer with a reply per line, so they want `more`
# and are refused without it; they end with a reply whose field is null, so that
# the end needs no lookahead and a run with nothing to say is not a special
# case.
#
# There is no way to ask for another root: which tree is configured is decided
# by whoever starts the service, and never by the caller.  The paths that the
# methods take are paths on the machine that answers; the one thing that travels
# is a bundle, which is a file that carries its own signature, and what it can
# reach in the system is decided by the machine that installs it.
interface org.detc.Manager

# A type of object that the system has.  This is the whole vocabulary: a call
# that names anything else is refused, and a type added to the system is added
# here.  It is not the type of a resource, which is whatever a provider chooses
# to implement and travels as a plain string.
type ObjectType (probe, template, resource, provider, variable)

# An object of the system: what it is, the name that addresses it, and the file
# it was read from.
type Object (
  type: ObjectType,
  name: string,
  source: string
)

# Whether an object can be instantiated, and why not.
#
# A type, a method and an error share one namespace in an interface, so this is
# not called `Check`: the method that answers with it is.  The name of a type
# never travels, only its fields do, so the two are the same thing to a client.
type Verdict (
  name: string,
  error: ?string
)

# Something that happens, or happened, to an object.  The action is the word
# the line starts with; it is `error` when the change could not be made, and
# `skipped` when the object was not tried at all because something it requires
# was not applied -- in which case `summary` names the requirement, and the run
# does not count it as a failure.  A client must treat the action as an open
# set of words rather than an enumeration, which is why it is a string.
type Change (
  action: string,
  object: string,
  summary: ?string,
  error: ?string
)

# A probe of the system, and where it is mounted in the namespace.
type Probe (
  mount: string,
  path: string
)

# A run that changed the system, as the journal lists it.  Almost always an
# `apply`; a `Remove` with `purge` is the other one, since it deletes a
# configuration file rather than only changing what would be written.
type Run (
  id: int,
  time: string,
  command: string,
  summary: string
)

# A commit that a run left in the journal.
type Commit (
  id: string,
  summary: string
)

# A run that changed the system, in full.
type RunDetail (
  id: int,
  time: string,
  command: string,
  cause: string,
  found: ?Commit,
  applied: ?Commit,
  lines: []string
)

# The bundle that the system knows: what it calls itself, who signed it, where
# it was taken from, whether a copy of it was kept for the next boot, and
# whether what it holds is in the system now.  The signer is `unsigned` when the
# bundle carried no signature.
#
# The last two are not the same question.  The copy outlives the tmpfs that the
# content lives in, so a machine between the reboot and the restore answers with
# `persist` and without `installed`, and holds none of what the bundle carries.
type Bundle (
  name: string,
  version: string,
  signer: string,
  origin: string,
  persist: bool,
  installed: bool
)

# A variable, and the value it takes.  The value is written in the YAML syntax,
# so that a number is a number and a quoted one is a string.
type Assignment (
  key: string,
  value: string
)

# The variables that override the namespace of one call: the assignments, and
# the `key=value` shorthands, which are parsed on the machine that answers.
type Var (
  assignment: []Assignment,
  kv: []string
)

# The types of object that the system has.  Streaming.
method ListTypes() -> (type: ?ObjectType)

# The objects of the system, of one type or of all of them.  With `masked`, the
# objects that a zero byte file takes out of the ladder instead, whose `source`
# is then the file that masks them rather than the file it hides.  Those are
# the names that no other method can see, since the resolver reads a zero byte
# file as the object being absent.  Streaming.
method List(masked: bool, type: ?ObjectType) -> (object: ?Object)

# What a template would write in the system, the state a resource declares, or
# the program a probe or a provider is.  `name` addresses the object the way
# `List` reports it, and `type` says which type to look in when the name alone
# would be read as another one.  With `raw`, the file as it was written, before
# the namespace reaches it; a program is never expanded.
method Cat(
  name: string,
  type: ?ObjectType,
  raw: bool,
  var: Var
) -> (text: string)

# Whether the objects can be instantiated, one object or all of them.  Building
# the namespace runs the probes that the system has installed.  Streaming.
method Check(
  name: ?string,
  type: ?ObjectType,
  var: Var
) -> (check: ?Verdict)

# An object described for a person, from the comments at the head of its file.
# `name` and `type` address it the way `Cat` does.  For a provider the schema
# it publishes is appended to what its header says.
method Doc(name: string, type: ?ObjectType) -> (text: string)

# The schema that a provider publishes, as it writes it.  `name` is the type
# the provider implements or the path of the program, the way `Cat` addresses
# one.
method Schema(name: string) -> (text: string)

# The namespace as YAML: the given keys, or all of it when there are none.
method GetVariables(key: []string) -> (text: string)

# The probes that the system has installed.  Streaming.
method ListProbes() -> (probe: ?Probe)

# What one probe reports, as YAML.  The probe is named by its path, or by the
# tail of it.
method RunProbe(probe: string) -> (text: string)

# The runs that the journal holds, the most recent first, and only the ones
# that failed when asked.  Streaming.
method ListRuns(only_fails: bool) -> (run: ?Run)

# One run of `apply` in full, the most recent when no id is given.
method GetRun(id: ?int) -> (detail: RunDetail)

# What went wrong in one run, as the lines the journal kept.  Streaming.
method GetFailures(id: ?int) -> (line: ?string)

# Whether a bundle can be trusted and everything it carries can be installed.
# `bundle` is the file itself, base64 encoded, and `url` is one that the machine
# that answers fetches itself.  Exactly one of the two.  Streaming.
method VerifyBundle(bundle: ?string, url: ?string) -> (check: ?Verdict)

# The bundle that is installed, or the one that was kept when a reboot has taken
# the installed one away and nothing has restored it yet, and nothing when the
# machine knows neither.  Streaming.
method GetBundle() -> (bundle: ?Bundle)

# Bring the system to the state that its objects declare, one object or all of
# them.  With `dry_run`, say what would change and write nothing.  Streaming.
method Apply(
  name: ?string,
  type: ?ObjectType,
  dry_run: bool,
  var: Var
) -> (change: ?Change)

# Take objects away, named the way `List` reports them.  What is unlinked is
# the file that the ladder resolved, so taking one away uncovers whatever was
# under it: the `remains` action reports the file that answers for the name
# afterwards, and nothing is said when the name no longer addresses anything.
# A file that the distribution ships is not detc's to unlink, and neither is one
# that a bundle installed; `mask` writes the zero byte file in the
# administrator's prefix that the resolver reads as absent, which is what
# reaches both.
#
# An object that nothing answers for any more can leave something behind, and
# the `orphan` action reports it: for a template the file it instantiated, which
# keeps configuring the machine with nothing left to say where it came from; for
# a provider every resource of its type, which nothing can now apply; and for a
# resource whatever its provider did about it, which nothing manages now.  The
# summary of a template's orphan says whether the file is still as detc wrote
# it; a resource's says only that it is unmanaged, reading the real state being
# a run of every probe on the machine.  `purge` takes a template's file away as
# well, and only where it is: a file that was edited, or one whose template no
# longer renders and so cannot be compared, is named and left alone.
#
# `purge` is refused for every type but template, and for two reasons.  A probe,
# a provider and a variable document write nothing into the system.  A resource
# does leave something, but absence is a state a declaration asks for like any
# other, and reaching it is what `Apply` is: declare the resource absent, apply
# that, and take the declaration away once the system says so.
#
# A purge is the one part of a removal that the journal holds, being the one
# part that changes the system rather than what the system would do.  It is a
# single commit rather than the pair that `Apply` writes, a file that is gone
# having no before and after to compare, and `Report` names it under the
# `remove` command.  A removal without `purge` records nothing, and the next
# `Apply` records the difference.
#
# With `dry_run`, name what would be taken away.  A dry run cannot say what a
# removal would uncover, since that is a question about the ladder without the
# file in it, so it reports no `remains` and no verdict on an orphan -- with
# `purge` it still names the file that is at stake.  Streaming.
method Remove(
  names: []string,
  type: ?ObjectType,
  mask: bool,
  purge: bool,
  dry_run: bool
) -> (change: ?Change)

# Put masked objects back in the ladder, named the way `List` with `masked`
# reports them, or by the path of the zero byte file itself.  This is the other
# end of `Remove` with `mask`, and the only method that addresses an object the
# resolver reads as absent.
#
# The `unmask` action names the file that was unlinked, and one line follows
# saying what the ladder answers for the name now: `remains` with the file that
# was under the mask, `masked` when a second zero byte file was under the first
# and still hides it, or `absent` when the mask covered no file at all.
#
# A mask that the distribution ships is not detc's to unlink, and neither is one
# that a bundle installed; there is no second answer to offer for either, since
# a mask cannot itself be masked.
#
# With `dry_run`, name the mask that would be unlinked.  A dry run cannot say
# what putting the object back would uncover, since that is a question about the
# ladder without the mask in it.  Streaming.
method Unmask(
  names: []string,
  type: ?ObjectType,
  dry_run: bool
) -> (change: ?Change)

# Write the variables as drop-ins of the namespace.  They last until the next
# boot, unless `persist` keeps them, which also takes away the drop-in that the
# same variable had until then.  With `dry_run`, name the drop-ins that would
# be written and the ones that would be taken away.  Streaming.
method SetVariables(var: Var, persist: bool, dry_run: bool) -> (change: ?Change)

# Take away the drop-ins that `SetVariables` wrote for the given keys, the
# persisted ones as well as the ones that last until the next boot.  Only those
# are reached: a document that somebody wrote, or that a bundle installed, is
# never taken away here, so a key can still be set afterwards -- which is what
# the `remains` action reports, with the file that now answers for it, or `a
# probe` when the machine is the one reporting the value.  A key that no drop-in
# holds is not a failure.  With `dry_run`, name the drop-ins that would be taken
# away.  Streaming.
method UnsetVariables(key: []string, dry_run: bool) -> (change: ?Change)

# Merge a document of variables into the namespace and keep it, until the next
# boot or, with `persist`, for good.  The file is read on the machine that
# answers.  Streaming.
method MergeDocument(file: string, persist: bool, dry_run: bool) -> (change: ?Change)

# Install a bundle, taking away the one that was installed before it.  `bundle`
# is the file itself, base64 encoded, and `url` is one that the machine that
# answers fetches itself; exactly one of the two, or neither to install again
# the copy that `persist` left in the system.  With `apply`, the system is
# brought to the state that the bundle declares once it is installed.
# Streaming.
method InstallBundle(
  bundle: ?string,
  url: ?string,
  persist: bool,
  apply: bool,
  allow_unsigned: bool,
  dry_run: bool
) -> (change: ?Change)

# Take away the installed bundle, and the copy that was kept of it.  Streaming.
method RemoveBundle(dry_run: bool) -> (change: ?Change)

# The service was started read-only, and the method changes the system.
error ReadOnly()

# The method answers with a stream, and the call did not ask for one.
error ExpectedMore()

# The call failed, with the message that `detc` reports.
error Failed(message: string)
